# React component style

## Always type components with `FC<Props>`

When declaring a React functional component, type it with `FC<Props>` from `react` — not inline props on a `function` declaration.

Prefer:

```tsx
import type { FC } from 'react';

interface FieldingStatsTableProps {
  title: string;
  rows: Array<{ playerId: string; label: string; stats: PlayerGameStats }>;
}

export const FieldingStatsTable: FC<FieldingStatsTableProps> = ({ title, rows }) => {
  // ...
};
```

Avoid:

```tsx
interface FieldingStatsTableProps {
  title: string;
  rows: Array<{ playerId: string; label: string; stats: PlayerGameStats }>;
}

export function FieldingStatsTable({ title, rows }: FieldingStatsTableProps) {
  // ...
}
```

Applies to exported components and file-local helpers/sub-components.

## Notes

- Import `FC` as type-only: `import type { FC } from 'react'` (or add `FC` to an existing `import type` from `react`).
- Declare props interface/type above the component, named `<ComponentName>Props`.
- Same convention for default and named exports.
